      "questionHtml": "<div class=\"res_question_text\">\n<div class=\"q-source-tag\"><strong>⚡ GO Classes CS 2027 Style | Turing Machines</strong></div>\n<p>Let $L$ be a language such that both $L$ and its complement $\\overline{L}$ are Recursively Enumerable (RE). Which of the following is true?</p>\n<div class=\"solution-explanation-panel\" style=\"margin-top: 1.25rem; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px; background: rgba(99, 102, 241, 0.08); border-left: 4px solid var(--accent-primary);\">\n  <strong style=\"color: var(--accent-primary);\">💡 Official Solution & Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong>\n  <div style=\"margin-top: 0.5rem; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;\">\n    By Post's Theorem, a language $L$ is Recursive (decidable) if and only if both $L$ and $\\overline{L}$ are Recursively Enumerable (Turing-recognizable).\n  </div>\n</div>\n</div>",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "html": "<span>$L$ must be Context-Sensitive</span>",
          "textHtml": "<span>$L$ must be Context-Sensitive</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "html": "<span>$L$ must be Recursive (Decidable)</span>",
          "textHtml": "<span>$L$ must be Recursive (Decidable)</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "html": "<span>$L$ may be undecidable</span>",
          "textHtml": "<span>$L$ may be undecidable</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "html": "<span>$\\overline{L}$ is not Turing-recognizable</span>",
          "textHtml": "<span>$\\overline{L}$ is not Turing-recognizable</span>"
        }
      ],
